The guards in (2.3) have exact source meaning. $u>1$ is precisely $b>0$, and $0<N/L<1$ is precisely $0<r<1$. For $d\ne24$, the two nonzero odd readings force $A\ne0$ and $B\ne0$, so $AB\ne0$ removes no legal world. Equation (3.4) divides by the odd samples of the factor polynomial in (3.3), equivalently by the corresponding nonzero gain samples; their nonvanishing is already forced by the acquired readings in this drift range, and the exceptional drift is excluded separately in Section 5. Beyond the explicitly fixed $g(7)=0$, no extra condition is imposed on sampled gain values, and no extra nonvanishing assumption is imposed on a gain leading coefficient, root difference, direction coordinate, singularity coordinate, or companion denominator. Lower gain degrees, repeated roots, arbitrary signs, zero drift before the separate exclusion, and singular companions remain whenever the original source conditions hold. The only added condition is $g(7)=0$, together with the common-drift route used in the classification.

